WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:00.750
llama.

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:06.323
cpp is a lightweight local inference project for running large language models on personal computers,

00:00:06.323 --> 00:00:07.809
workstations, servers, and homelabs.

00:00:07.809 --> 00:00:13.753
It is popular because it makes local model execution practical without requiring a large managed platform.

00:00:13.753 --> 00:00:18.583
A user can load a compatible model file, send prompts to the runtime,

00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:22.298
and experiment with language model behavior on hardware they control.

00:00:22.298 --> 00:00:26.013
Local inference is different from using a hosted AI API.

00:00:26.013 --> 00:00:30.472
With a hosted service, the provider operates the model, runtime, scaling layer,

00:00:30.472 --> 00:00:32.329
and much of the infrastructure.

00:00:32.329 --> 00:00:33.072
With llama.

00:00:33.072 --> 00:00:37.530
cpp, the user controls the model file, runtime version, hardware, performance settings,

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.645
and deployment pattern.

00:00:38.645 --> 00:00:41.617
That control can improve privacy, support offline workflows,

00:00:41.617 --> 00:00:45.332
reduce dependency on external services, and make experimentation more transparent.

00:00:45.332 --> 00:00:45.982
llama.

00:00:45.982 --> 00:00:50.441
cpp is closely associated with GGUF model files and local model serving.

00:00:50.441 --> 00:00:55.642
GGUF packages model weights and metadata in a format that compatible runtimes can load.

00:00:55.642 --> 00:00:56.292
llama.

00:00:56.292 --> 00:00:59.636
cpp can run models interactively, expose local server endpoints,

00:00:59.636 --> 00:01:03.351
and act as a foundation for web UIs or applications.

00:01:03.351 --> 00:01:07.809
The file, runtime, prompt formatting, and hardware all have to fit together.

00:01:07.809 --> 00:01:10.781
It helps to separate three levels of use.

00:01:10.781 --> 00:01:15.239
Running a model means loading it and getting output from a prompt.

00:01:15.239 --> 00:01:20.441
Serving a model means making that inference available through an API or local service.

00:01:20.441 --> 00:01:24.899
Building an application means wrapping that service with user workflows, data handling,

00:01:24.899 --> 00:01:27.128
access control, logging, and reliability expectations.

00:01:27.128 --> 00:01:28.614
Getting started with llama.

00:01:28.614 --> 00:01:32.980
cpp is about learning all three levels without losing sight of repeatability and safety.
